  · test gap: top-quartile
Likely tests  (no sibling, __tests__, .test, .spec … matched)
Related files (no neighbourhood signal)
```

Three things shape the refactor:

- **Blast radius 0 importers, churn low, one author** — nothing downstream depends on this component's current shape, so a structural rewrite is cheap. This is the safest possible time to do it.
- **Test gap top-quartile with zero candidate test files** — all three findings carry `test_gap: 1.0`. There's no safety net, and the accessibility fix is exactly the kind that regresses silently. The refactor needs a test, not just a diff.
- **`tailwind.config.ts` exists with a real token scale** (`brand`, `surface`, `ink`) — so `design_token_escape` isn't "this project has no design system," it's "this file drove around the one it has."

I'm proposing below rather than editing: this file is an eval fixture whose comment header declares which detectors it's supposed to trip, so changing it in place would break the fixture set.

## Proposed `src/Card.tsx`

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  sidebar?: React.ReactNode;
  /** When provided, the whole card becomes a single activatable control. */
  onSelect?: () => void;
};

export function Card({ children, sidebar, onSelect }: CardProps) {
  const shell = (
    <div className="@container w-full max-w-4xl rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-card">
      <div className="flex flex-col gap-4 @md:flex-row">
        <div className="min-w-0 flex-1 text-lg">{children}</div>
        {sidebar ? (
          <aside className="text-muted min-w-0 text-sm @md:w-60 @md:shrink-0">
            {sidebar}
          </aside>
        ) : null}
      </div>
    </div>
  );

  if (!onSelect) return shell;

  return (
    <button
      type="button"
      onClick={onSelect}
      className="block w-full max-w-4xl rounded-lg text-left focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus"
    >
      {shell}
    </button>
  );
}
```

And the token additions this requires — four of the nine raw values have no token to map onto yet, so part of the fix is extending the system, not just swapping literals:

```ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      ink: "#111111",
      muted: "#666666",   // was the #666666 sidebar literal
      subtle: "#cccccc",  // was the #cccccc borderColor
      focus: "#1a73e8",   // was the #1a73e8 outlineColor
    },
    boxShadow: {
      card: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2)", // was the raw rgb() shadow
    },
  },
};
```

## Each change tied to its finding

| Change | Finding | Why it closes it |
|---|---|---|
| `<div onClick>` → real `<button type="button">` wrapper; `onClick` becomes an optional `onSelect` prop | **crime_00001 · Accessible Interaction Risk** (medium, conf 0.85) — *line 10: `<div>` with onClick has no role / aria-label / tabIndex* | Matches the finding's own `fix_shape`: "use semantic elements; add ARIA only when semantics don't fit." A `<button>` is focusable, Enter/Space-activatable, and announced as a button with zero ARIA. The optional prop is the second half — the fixture's `console.log("clicked")` is a phantom handler, and a card with no real action shouldn't be focusable at all. `focus-visible:outline-focus` restores the visible focus ring the old `outlineColor` was gesturing at. |
| All 9 `style` literals → token classes: `#0a0a0a`→`text-ink`, `#cccccc`→`border-subtle`, `#1a73e8`→`outline-focus`, `rgb(0,0,0)` shadow→`shadow-card`, `#333333`/`#666666`→`text-ink`/`text-muted`, `padding: 24`→`p-6` | **crime_00002 · Design Token Escape** (low, conf 0.75) — *9 hard-coded style values across 4 kinds* | The agent-risk story in the summary is the real one: an agent extending this file copies `#ff00aa` forward and the escape spreads. Routing every value through the theme means the next edit inherits the system by default. **One value needs a human decision, not a mapping:** `background: "#ff00aa"` is hot magenta against a `surface` of `#f7f7fa` — that isn't a token drift, it's off-system entirely. I've assumed it was placeholder and mapped it to `bg-surface`; if it was deliberate, it needs a named token (`bg-accent`) rather than a literal. |
| `width: 800` → `w-full max-w-4xl`; `height: 600` dropped; inner `720`/`240` → `@container` + `flex-col` → `@md:flex-row` with `flex-1` / `@md:w-60`; `fontSize: 18` → `text-lg` | **crime_00003 · Responsive Fragility** (low, conf 0.65) — *width 800px, width 720px, fontSize 18px, no `@media` in file* | Follows the finding's `fix_shape` — "drive layout from container queries or a fluid scale, not pixel media queries." Container queries are the right call over `@media` here specifically *because* blast radius is 0 importers: nobody knows yet where this card gets placed, so it should respond to its slot, not the viewport. `w-full max-w-4xl` is the finding's own suggested `max-width` + `width: 100%` pattern. Dropping the fixed `height: 600` is a bonus — it wasn't flagged, but a fixed height with arbitrary `children` clips content. |

## The trap worth naming

There's a cheap way to "fix" `design_token_escape` that I deliberately avoided: `className="bg-[#ff00aa]"`. That moves the literal out of the `style` object the detector reads while changing nothing about the actual escape. If the scan goes green that way, the tool got gamed, not satisfied. Same for `responsive_fragility` and `w-[800px]`.

## Verification

`crimes scan` should return 0 findings on this file — but that's necessary, not sufficient, given the trap above. To close the top-quartile test gap, add `src/Card.test.tsx`:

- renders a `role="button"` **only** when `onSelect` is passed, and nothing focusable when it isn't (guards crime_00001 against regressing to a `<div>`)
- fires `onSelect` on `Enter` and `Space`, not just click — the keyboard path is what the original silently dropped
- asserts no `style` prop on the rendered tree, which pins both crime_00002 and crime_00003 as an actual invariant rather than a one-time cleanup
